WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.160
Okay.

00:04.200 --> 00:10.320
So now moving on to the any operator so we can go and use the any operator in order to compare a value

00:10.320 --> 00:13.400
if it matches any value from a list.

00:13.400 --> 00:18.920
So that means we can go and use it in order to check whether a condition is true for at least one of

00:18.920 --> 00:20.520
the values in a list.

00:20.560 --> 00:25.280
Okay, so now let's check quickly the syntax of the subquery using the any and all operators.

00:25.280 --> 00:30.600
So as we learned before we can go and use a subquery inside the Where clause in order to filter the

00:30.600 --> 00:37.000
main query using like the comparison operators like here less than now, the syntax of the any operator

00:37.040 --> 00:40.600
is that you're going to go and use the comparison operator.

00:40.600 --> 00:44.080
And after that immediately you use the keyword any.

00:44.120 --> 00:48.640
And for the all operator it's going to be exactly the same where you're going to go and put after the

00:48.640 --> 00:50.920
comparison operator the keyword all.

00:50.960 --> 00:52.720
So the syntax is very simple.

00:52.720 --> 00:54.560
We just add those keywords.

00:54.560 --> 00:56.640
So let's practice using the following task.

00:56.680 --> 01:03.790
Find female employees whose salaries are greater than the salaries of any male employee.

01:03.790 --> 01:07.510
So that means we want to go and compare the salaries between the male and female.

01:07.510 --> 01:13.910
And specifically, we are searching for female employees whose salary is greater than at least one male

01:13.910 --> 01:14.470
employee.

01:14.470 --> 01:16.150
So let's solve it step by step.

01:16.190 --> 01:25.750
Let's go and start selecting few informations, like for example the employee ID and first name gender

01:26.110 --> 01:30.470
salary from the table sales employees.

01:30.470 --> 01:32.030
So let's go and execute it.

01:32.030 --> 01:33.950
So now we have like five employees.

01:33.990 --> 01:36.470
Three of them are male and two are female.

01:36.470 --> 01:41.990
So now since we want to compare the data between male and female, let's go and create two queries.

01:41.990 --> 01:45.070
The first one is filtering the database on the gender.

01:45.830 --> 01:48.110
So the first one is for the female.

01:48.110 --> 01:51.150
So and we can go and remove this information over here.

01:51.190 --> 01:54.670
Let me just make this a little bit smaller and zoom out.

01:54.670 --> 01:58.790
And the second query is going to be the exact opposite.

01:58.790 --> 02:02.590
Let's go and get employee informations for the male.

02:02.990 --> 02:04.670
So let's go and execute it.

02:04.670 --> 02:04.690
Skillsets.

02:04.730 --> 02:08.450
Now the first results are the female employees and the second one are.

02:08.490 --> 02:11.370
So now for the first result is for the female employees.

02:11.370 --> 02:14.130
And the second one is for the male employees.

02:14.130 --> 02:15.810
So now what do we need in the output.

02:15.810 --> 02:17.530
We need the female employees.

02:17.530 --> 02:21.610
That means this is going to be our main query.

02:22.010 --> 02:24.810
So we are focusing on the female employees.

02:24.810 --> 02:28.170
And we are using the male employees only as a filter.

02:28.370 --> 02:31.530
And what we need, we need only the salary informations.

02:31.530 --> 02:33.650
That's why we can prepare it like this.

02:34.410 --> 02:38.330
I will just put everything in one line to make it clear.

02:38.490 --> 02:40.850
So this is going to be our sub query.

02:40.850 --> 02:45.810
So now we're going to go and work with the main query where we're going to add one more filter where

02:45.810 --> 02:48.290
we're going to filter the data based on the salary.

02:48.290 --> 02:48.610
Right.

02:48.610 --> 02:52.450
So we're going to say if the salary is greater than.

02:52.490 --> 02:55.490
And now we need the values from the subquery.

02:55.490 --> 02:55.810
Right.

02:55.810 --> 02:57.290
So this is our sub query.

02:57.570 --> 02:58.650
We're going to put it like this.

02:58.650 --> 03:02.090
And don't forget about the parenthesis at the start and at the end.

03:02.130 --> 03:05.610
And I would like still to have those two queries.

03:06.290 --> 03:08.160
So let's go and execute it.

03:08.160 --> 03:09.440
And now we will get an error.

03:09.440 --> 03:13.840
And that's because our subquery is returning multiple rows.

03:13.880 --> 03:15.360
And this is not acceptable.

03:15.360 --> 03:17.160
We are using the comparison operator.

03:17.160 --> 03:22.000
And SQL expects from the subquery to have scalar subquery.

03:22.000 --> 03:23.760
So only one single value.

03:23.760 --> 03:29.520
But now in order to solve this issue we can go and use the logical operators either all or any.

03:29.520 --> 03:34.840
So now since we are saying it's enough for the salary of the female employee to be higher than at least

03:34.880 --> 03:38.520
one male employee, we will go with the operator any.

03:38.560 --> 03:43.080
So let's go after the comparison operator and have the keyword any.

03:43.120 --> 03:45.160
And let's go and execute it again.

03:45.200 --> 03:50.960
And now as you can see in the output, we got only one female employee where her salary is higher to

03:51.000 --> 03:52.960
one of those male employees.

03:52.960 --> 03:59.680
So let me just go and get the first name as well from the second query just to have it like this.

03:59.680 --> 04:05.440
So now if you go and compare the salary of Mari, it is not higher than Michael, but it is higher than

04:05.480 --> 04:06.800
Frank and Kevin.

04:06.800 --> 04:08.980
And since we are using the any operator.

04:08.980 --> 04:13.060
It's enough for Mary to have salary higher to one of those values.

04:13.060 --> 04:17.260
In this case, it's higher than both Frank and Kevin, and the condition is fulfilled.

04:17.260 --> 04:19.940
That's why we are getting the Mary and the other female.

04:19.940 --> 04:21.020
Let me just check.

04:21.580 --> 04:22.500
Do we have else?

04:22.500 --> 04:23.540
So we have Carol.

04:23.580 --> 04:27.540
His salary is less than all the salaries of the male employees.

04:27.540 --> 04:31.300
So it must be at least higher than one of the male employees.

04:31.300 --> 04:33.340
So with us, we have solved the task, right.

04:33.380 --> 04:33.660
All right.

04:33.660 --> 04:36.060
So now we have another operator that is similar.

04:36.060 --> 04:38.140
We call it the all operator.

04:38.140 --> 04:44.140
We can go and use it in order to compare a value if it matches all values in a list.

04:44.140 --> 04:45.580
So that means we can go and use it.

04:45.580 --> 04:48.820
If we need to check whether a condition is true.

04:48.860 --> 04:50.660
Every value in a list.

04:50.660 --> 04:53.500
I know that might sound a little bit complicated, but don't worry about it.

04:53.500 --> 04:54.580
We can have examples.

04:54.620 --> 05:01.380
Now let's say that our task says find female employees whose salary are greater than the salaries of

05:01.380 --> 05:03.780
all male employees.

05:03.780 --> 05:06.540
So that means now the condition is more restrictive.

05:06.780 --> 05:13.090
Mary should now has a salary higher than every male employee, so it should be higher.

05:13.290 --> 05:16.450
To all those values that we have from the male employees.

05:16.450 --> 05:20.610
And of course, in this scenario, it's not because we have Michael.

05:20.650 --> 05:23.250
Mary has less salaries than Michael.

05:23.250 --> 05:28.010
And this is a problem because Mary should has higher salary than everyone.

05:28.010 --> 05:29.170
So let's go and try it.

05:29.170 --> 05:35.530
If I go and write here all and let's go and execute it, you will see we will not find any results that

05:35.530 --> 05:36.970
fulfill this requirement.

05:36.970 --> 05:42.530
So we don't have any female employee or her salary is higher than all male employees.

05:42.530 --> 05:44.610
And that's because we have a very small data sets.

05:44.650 --> 05:49.570
So this is how we use all and any operators in our subqueries in SQL.

05:49.610 --> 05:49.970
All right.

05:49.970 --> 05:55.530
So with that we have covered almost everything about how to use the subqueries in different locations

05:55.530 --> 05:56.410
and clauses.

05:56.410 --> 05:58.930
But we didn't talk about the exist operator.

05:58.930 --> 06:03.610
And that's because I would like you to understand a very important concept in the subqueries, where

06:03.610 --> 06:09.970
we have two different types of the subqueries based on the dependencies, the non-correlated and correlated

06:09.970 --> 06:10.890
subqueries.

06:10.890 --> 06:13.570
And after that we're going to go back to the exist operator.
